  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(4,5),$ so we have $f(4)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(4)=-2,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+(-2)(x-4) \\\\  & =5-2x+8 \\\\  & =-2x+13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(4.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=4.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.4) & ≈L(4.4) \\\\  & =-2(4.4)+13 \\\\  & =-8.8+13 \\\\  & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(2,5),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=4,$ and therefore we have an underestimate.",
